<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thoth</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Djehuty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thoth was the ancient Egyptian god of writing, wisdom, the moon and reckoning, shown as an ibis-headed man or as a baboon. He was believed to have invented hieroglyphs, kept the records of the gods and written down the verdict when the hearts of the dead were weighed. His chief cult centre was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermopolis">Hermopolis</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> identified him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermes">Hermes</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thoth.jpg" alt="Thoth" width="320" height="618" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Dahl · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thoth.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
